<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Resilience as the baseline. | Canada2080"
    description="Dr Michael Donaldson on systems that do not deliver, an innovation mindset, and resilience as a baseline, from his cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Capacity · 1:55 p.m."
    headline="Resilience as the baseline."
    name="Dr Michael Donaldson"
    bio="Dr Michael Donaldson is Principal Architect and Designer at DWA / Future Workshop. His practice spans architecture, strategic design, and designed futures."
    profileUrl="https://www.designworkshop.ca/studio"
    profileLabel="Design Workshop"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5425.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Dr Michael Donaldson speaking at the podium, in a white shirt and white glasses."
    photos={[
        {
            src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5425.jpg",
            alt: "Dr Michael Donaldson speaking at the podium, in a white shirt and white glasses.",
            caption: "At the podium.",
        },
    ]}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Why do we keep doing what does not work? He has the question, and he
        will not let it go.
    </p>
    <p>
        He agreed with Greg Hart, and said he has lived that problem for ten
        years at the scale of private practice. He is an architect. He has
        built a business and watched the systems he has to use. Most of them,
        he said, deliver almost none of the outcome they were set up to
        deliver.
    </p>
    <p>
        The field promised a creative practice, a practical one, and
        problem-solving. What he found was a long argument with where his
        energy goes. He comes back to Greg's point. Systems deliver what they
        deliver. Why do we allow that to continue? He does not have the answer.
        He has the questions.
    </p>
    <p>
        His clients include some of the largest controllers of commercial
        property, and some of the largest investors in the Canadian economy and
        its infrastructure. They still avoid risk, and they still think
        short-term, even when the asset is supposed to come into its value in
        10 or 20 years. That horizon, he said, is not really different from
        expecting a return in six months.
    </p>
    <p>
        He asks the people delivering those projects why they believe the work
        will do what they say, and where their agency is. There is usually no
        answer.
    </p>
    <p>
        The work he wants for the country is to unpack why we accept what does
        not deliver, and to stop accepting it. He called that an innovation
        mindset: help people become unwilling to accept what is not optimal,
        and help them encourage that kind of thinking.
    </p>
    <p>
        Expo 67 comes up in his family. Both of his parents volunteered. He
        treats it as part of a founding story he wants the country to keep
        returning to: what is positive, the way Canadians tell a story, and the
        way they come together when it is difficult.
    </p>
    <p>
        He described the moment as the negative side of an American
        hyper-capitalism, and said Canada is already inside it. Data has been
        extracted. He does not think patience is available anymore. A resilient
        economy and a resilient society have to be built on many levels. Canada
        is good at that when it has to be, and it does not do it enough.
    </p>
    <p>His test for a difficult time.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The power is out for four days, in the depth of winter, and no food
            is coming in. Would you know what to do?
        </li>
        <li>How would you know how to talk to people?</li>
        <li>
            How would you know how to have a conversation about solving the
            problem? Some people will. Not everybody will.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        He pointed to countries that live with obvious security threats, and
        asked how Canada can be more like them: decentralize, become more
        resilient, and weave that expectation into the culture of what we do.
        He knows it is a strange thing for an architect to say. The problems
        are real, whatever the future looks like. He hopes people start asking,
        and that an individual can push back when something does not make
        sense.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                So, I think I 100% agree with everything that Greg just said,
                and I think that over the last 10 years, I've been living those
                problems at a very small scale, and that is the scale of
                private practice. I'm an architect, having been through this
                full cycle of developing a business and trying to understand
                how effective the systems that I have to engage with really are
                in delivering the outcomes that they're supposed to be
                delivering. The reality is most of them deliver virtually none
                of what is set up as an ideal outcome at the beginning of that
                process.
            </p>
            <p>
                So what I've found over those years is that I've constantly had
                to revisit why that happens and where my energy goes. I can be
                honest with myself about what I found. The starting principles
                of going into a field like architecture, which assumes that
                there's a creative aspect to it, that there is a practical
                aspect to it, that there's problem-solving, and that all of
                those things that come into it are what you're really up to,
                has been a frustrating and interesting process of trying to
                understand what that means.
            </p>
            <p>
                You know, when Greg is talking about systems delivering what
                they're delivering, I completely agree, and I keep coming back
                to the central question of: why is it that we allow that to
                continue? Like, what is it about something that doesn't work
                that keeps us doing things that don't work? I don't have any
                answers, but I do have a lot of questions. So, I think that's
                what I'll use my few minutes here for.
            </p>
            <p>
                One of the interesting things about the work that I've been
                able to do is that we engage with really large client
                organizations. These are some of the largest controllers of
                commercial property and largest investors into the Canadian
                economy and infrastructure, and they still behave in the same
                way that we understand them to, which is an incredible aversion
                to risk, and also a very short-term investment horizon, even
                for things that look long-term. Like, if you're talking about a
                piece of property that may come into its value 10 or 20 years
                from now, that sounds long-term, but it isn't really any
                different from the thinking of an initial return on an
                investment that you make that might come to fruition in six
                months.
            </p>
            <p>
                So that problem keeps coming up over and over again. Even
                through asking the people that are also trying to deliver those
                projects and products, why is it that you think that this is
                going to do this for you? Where is your agency being exerted
                into this process? There isn't really usually an answer.
            </p>
            <p>
                So I think this feels like one of the most important things we
                need to do in this country: to start unpacking that question of
                why we accept things that don't really work, or why we engage
                with things that are not really delivering what we want them
                to, and that we don't accept that. This is really what I think.
                I'll mention this as the innovation mindset. How do you help
                people to arrive at an unwillingness to accept something that
                is not optimal, and how can you help people to encourage that
                type of thinking?
            </p>
            <p>
                Again, if we come back to what other people have said, the idea
                of Expo 67 is something that comes up in my family a lot. Both
                of my parents volunteered at Expo 67, and they both were part
                of that story. I've always had this sense of that as part of
                the founding story of Canada, of the positive things about the
                country that I really want to make sure that we're constantly
                going back to. So I feel like it's really important that we
                start talking more about what is positive about the country,
                things that make us able to tell stories in a certain way and
                able to come together in times of difficulty in a way that
                maybe other people can't as much as they would like to.
            </p>
            <p>
                We're in a strange time where we're being confronted with the
                negative side of an American hyper-capitalism, and we kind of
                know that we are already part of the matrix, right? We've been
                having data extracted from us. Sorry, I'm just clicking things
                here. And we need to try to figure out how we can get everybody
                in our own ecosystem talking about things that matter now, in a
                way that we just can't be patient anymore. We have the need to
                develop a resilient economy and a resilient society on a whole
                bunch of different levels. We are really good at this when we
                need to be, but we don't do it enough.
            </p>
            <p>
                And I'll ask some really simple questions about how well
                prepared we are for difficult times. Would you know what to do
                if the power went out for four days in your city in the depth
                of winter and there was no food coming in? How would you know
                how to talk to people? How would you know how to have a
                conversation about problem-solving? There are individuals that
                are going to be very good at that, but not everybody is good at
                that.
            </p>
            <p>
                We can look to countries that are dealing with obvious real
                security threats and ask: how can we be a little bit more like
                them in terms of how we can decentralize things, how we can
                become a little bit more resilient, and how we can build a
                system that has that as a baseline expectation culturally woven
                into everything we do?
            </p>
            <p>
                So that may be a strange thing for an architect to talk about,
                but at the end of the day, we know that these things are real
                now. We know that we have to solve all these problems
                regardless of what the future looks like. And I really hope
                that we're able to start asking those questions and that, at an
                individual level, we can start pushing back when something
                doesn't make sense.
            </p>
            <p>
                So again, I have no answers to that, but I do have the big
                question and I can't let it go. So I'll just leave it there.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
